# Edge Impulse Model Required

This project requires an Edge Impulse model file named `model.eim`.

## How to Create Your Own Model

### 1. Create an Edge Impulse Account

Go to https://edgeimpulse.com/ and create a free account.

### 2. Create Impulse

Configure your impulse with these settings:

**Image Data:**
- **Image width**: 224
- **Image height**: 224
- **Resize mode**: Fit shortest axis

**Processing Block:**
- **Name**: Image
- **Input**: Image

**Learning Block:**
- **Model**: YOLOv5
- **Output**: Your object classes (e.g., "elf", "person")

Click **Save Impulse**.

### 3. Collect and Label Image Samples

Upload or capture images with objects to detect (e.g., "elf", "person").

**Labeling Requirements:**
- Draw bounding boxes around each object in your images
- Label each box with the correct class name
- Aim for at least 50+ images per class for good accuracy
- Include various angles, lighting conditions, and distances

### 4. Configure Image Processing Block

Navigate to **Image** in the left sidebar.

**Parameters:**
- **Color depth**: RGB

Click **Save parameters**, then **Generate features**.

### 5. Configure and Train YOLOv5 Model

Navigate to **Object Detection (YOLOv5)** in the left sidebar.

**Training Configuration:**

```yaml
Training:
  Training processor: GPU
  Number of training cycles: 100
  Learning rate: 0.001
  Model size: Pico
  Use pretrained weights: ✓ Enabled
  Batch size: 16

Advanced Settings:
  Validation set size: 20%
  Profile int8 model: ✓ Enabled
  Architecture type: No attention with ReLU
  Spatial Augmentation: Low
  Color space Augmentation: Low
  Early stopping start epoch: 10
```

**Detailed Parameter Explanations:**

- **Training processor**: GPU (required for YOLOv5, significantly faster than CPU)
- **Training cycles**: 100 epochs (balance between training time and accuracy)
- **Learning rate**: 0.001 (standard for YOLOv5 fine-tuning with pretrained weights)
- **Model size**: Pico (smallest variant, 682KB, ideal for edge devices)
- **Pretrained weights**: Uses transfer learning from COCO dataset (80 common objects)
- **Validation split**: 20% of data reserved for validation during training
- **INT8 profiling**: Enables quantization analysis (optional for deployment)
- **Architecture**: No attention with ReLU (optimized for speed)
- **Spatial augmentation**: Low (prevents overfitting on small datasets)
- **Color augmentation**: Low (maintains color consistency)
- **Early stopping**: Stops training at epoch 10 if validation accuracy plateaus

Click **Start training** and wait for completion (~15-30 minutes).

**Expected Results:**
- **Mean Average Precision (mAP)**: >70% for good detection
- **Inference time**: ~58ms on AARCH64 (float32)
- **Model size**: 682KB (YOLOv5 Pico)

### 6. Test Model Performance

Navigate to **Model testing** in the left sidebar.

Click **Classify all** to test against your test set.

**Review metrics:**
- Overall mAP should be >70%
- Check per-class precision and recall
- Visually inspect test images for detection quality

### 7. Deploy to Arduino Uno Q

Navigate to **Deployment** in the left sidebar.

**Deployment Configuration:**

```yaml
Target: Linux (AARCH64)
Optimization: Unoptimized (float32)
```

**Important Notes:**
- Use **AARCH64** (ARM64 architecture) for Arduino Uno Q
- Choose **Unoptimized (float32)** for best accuracy
  - YOLOv5 Pico is already very small (682KB)
  - Float32 provides better detection accuracy
  - ~58ms inference time is acceptable for real-time use
  - INT8 quantization available if speed is critical

**Build Options:**
- Enable EON Compiler: ✓ Recommended
- Model format: `.eim` (Edge Impulse Model)

Click **Build** and wait (~2-3 minutes).

### 8. Download and Place the Model

**Download:**
- File will be named: `<project-name>-linux-aarch64-v<version>.eim`
- Size: ~50-70MB
- Rename it to: `model.eim`

**Place the Model:**

Copy to the `arduino-elf-webui` directory:

```bash
cp /path/to/your/model.eim arduino-elf-webui/
```

**Deployment Options:**

**Option A: Volume Mount (Recommended)**
```bash
# Copy model to device
scp model.eim arduino@<device-ip>:/var/local/assets/

# The docker-compose.yml mounts /var/local/assets automatically
# The start.sh script checks this location first
```

**Option B: Include in Container**
```bash
# Place model in arduino-elf-webui/ directory before building
# Model will be bundled in Docker image (increases image size)
```

## Model Requirements

- **Type**: Object detection (YOLOv5)
- **Architecture**: YOLOv5 Pico (682KB)
- **Format**: `.eim` (Edge Impulse Model)
- **Target**: Linux AARCH64 (ARM64)
- **Input size**: 224×224 RGB
- **Optimization**: Unoptimized (float32)
- **Performance**: ~58ms inference (~17 FPS)
- **Expected filename**: `model.eim`

## Performance Expectations

**On Arduino Uno Q (ARM Cortex-A53):**
- Inference time: ~58ms per frame
- Frame rate: ~17 FPS
- Model size: 682KB
- Memory usage: ~300MB (Docker container)
- CPU usage: 60-80% during inference

## Build Instructions

After placing your `model.eim` file:

```bash
docker build -t arduino-elf-webui .
```

For more information, see the main README.md
